*.pyc
ifi-policy-minimal.txt

# The translator's OWN output, wherever it lands under examples/. Blanket
# rather than the four names that used to be tracked, so a newly generated
# artifact cannot leak back in through a `git add .` -- the README's own
# example writes `examples/ifi.html`, which was never tracked and never
# ignored either.
#
# THEY WERE TRACKED AND THEY WENT STALE, which is the reason (TODO item 22,
# owner decision 2026-09-21). `ifi-policy-reachability.{html,csv}` were last
# written in 2021 and no run since reproduces them: the CSV header was
# `,1,2,3,4,5,6` where the translator emits role names, and the HTML named
# roles `ifi-policy.txt` no longer defines, with CSS classes `to_html` no
# longer emits. Four years of checked-in output that nothing regenerated and
# nothing verified -- CLOUD_BENCH_PLAN.md §1.8's complaint exactly. Every
# example is one command away (`policy_translator.py -w -o out.html
# examples/ifi-policy.txt`), so there is nothing to keep.
#
# The sources are `.txt` and the stylesheet is `.css`, so neither pattern can
# match an input. `policies.css` in particular is hand-written and stays
# tracked: the generated HTML links to it, which makes it an INPUT to the
# rendering rather than a product of it.
examples/*.html
examples/*.csv
